  "ruling": {
    "ruling": "PARTIAL_REFUND",
    "amount_sgd": 0.94,
    "driver_compensation_sgd": null,
    "referrals": [],
    "confidence": 0.95,
    "explanation": "Summary for both parties: the rider is owed part of what was asked, and 0.94 SGD is returned.\n\nThe deviation. POL-RD-22 fixes the deviation on the platform's own recorded distances, not on the GPS trace: the recommended route was 11.8 km and the vehicle was driven 13.1 km (EV-TRIP-05019), a deviation of 1.30 km. That is 0.30 km beyond the 1.0 km tolerance, so the within-tolerance rows of the matrix cannot apply, and it is well inside POL-RD-25's 2 km limit for an AI ruling. The trace (EV-GPS-05019) is used only for the two things POL-RD-22 allows it to show — which route was taken and when — and it shows the vehicle leaving the recommended route at the PIE / Adam Road exit at 17:04:15 and not rejoining until Bishan Road / Braddell Road at 17:16:09.\n\nJustification. The driver says the PIE was slow. POL-RD-23 requires traffic data for the recommended segment in the trip window, and the only such data on file (EV-ADVIS-05019-01) covers exactly this segment (PIE, Adam Road to CTE interchange) and window (16:22:25–17:52:25) but records type NONE, no incident, roadwork or closure, and speed bands 4–5 throughout. POL-RD-23 says in terms that a driver's assertion of traffic with no such data does not justify a reroute. The reroute is therefore not traffic-justified, and the driver's account of a slow PIE does not make it so.\n\nThe remedy. With the reroute not justified and the deviation over 1 km, row (d) of POL-RD-24 governs: the excess distance fare is refunded in full. That fare is the deviation distance times the base per-km rate without surge: 1.30 km x 0.72 SGD/km (per_km_rate_sgd, EV-FARE-05019) = 0.94 SGD, which is the figure the fare ledger itself derives. The rider's round figure of 3.00 SGD is not the measure POL-RD-24 provides, and the demand above 0.94 SGD is refused; to that extent the present charge stands. The ledger shows the fare of 17.81 SGD charged and refund_issued_sgd 0.00 (EV-FARE-05019), so 0.94 SGD is the sum to be returned, and this is a PARTIAL_REFUND, not a FULL_REFUND, because the rider is not owed the whole contested amount.\n\nOn the driver's 'I messaged the passenger and she said ok': it does not change the outcome. Every no-refund row of POL-RD-24 — (a), (b) and (c) — requires the reroute to be traffic-justified, and that condition fails. Row (d), which governs, contains no notification element at all, so the refund follows whether or not notice was given or agreed. I note for completeness that the chat log (EV-CHAT-05019) is impaired — weight_multiplier 0.6, issue code TRUNCATED_LOG, only 2 of the trip's 4 messages exported and both before pickup — so it neither proves notice nor disproves it; the ruling does not rest on it.\n\nWho decides. This is a case the AI can finish: neither profile carries a fraud flag (rider_profile.fraud_flags 0, driver_profile.fraud_flags 0), so POL-GEN-22's 0.95 uplift does not apply; at 3.00 SGD the dispute sits below the 10.00 SGD band; the deviation is within POL-RD-25's 2 km limit; and the record carries no safety indicator under POL-GEN-21. No fact that POL-RD-24 needs is left open, so POL-GEN-26 does not require escalation. Prior disputes, ratings, account age and the driver's single prior deviation flag are context only under POL-GEN-01 and decide nothing about this trip. Confidence 0.95, against the 0.80 bar that applies here; the deciding facts are carried by artifacts at weight 1.0 (EV-TRIP-05019, EV-FARE-05019, EV-ADVIS-05019-01). No referral clause applies to a route deviation in this version."
  },
  "discarded_claims": []
}
